1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,912
A large site often needs more than one sitemap.

2
00:00:03,912 --> 00:00:07,922
In this video, you combine sitemaps in a sitemap index, look at the

3
00:00:07,922 --> 00:00:13,580
Sitemaps Cache where Orchard Core stores them, and enable the cleanup that keeps that cache tidy.

4
00:00:18,180 --> 00:00:22,175
Search engines limit how large one sitemap can be, so a big site

5
00:00:22,175 --> 00:00:24,801
spreads its pages over several sitemaps.

6
00:00:24,801 --> 00:00:28,968
You can split them by content type, or with the Limit Items option,

7
00:00:28,968 --> 00:00:30,680
which has Skip and Take.

8
00:00:31,430 --> 00:00:34,489
A sitemap index is a sitemap of sitemaps.

9
00:00:34,489 --> 00:00:39,435
It lists the address of each sitemap it contains, with a last modified

10
00:00:39,435 --> 00:00:42,950
date, so a search engine needs only one address.

11
00:00:47,250 --> 00:00:51,360
Two sitemaps already exist on this site, Posts and Articles.

12
00:00:51,360 --> 00:00:54,785
Creating sitemaps is covered in a separate video.

13
00:00:54,785 --> 00:00:57,400
Let's combine these two in an index.

14
00:00:57,915 --> 00:01:04,341
Under Tools, Search Engine Optimization, Sitemap Indexes lists your indexes, and the list is empty.

15
00:01:04,341 --> 00:01:06,605
Let's choose Add Sitemap Index.

16
00:01:07,066 --> 00:01:08,800
Name the index Main.

17
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:13,469
The Permalink is its address, it is required, and it must end in

18
00:01:13,469 --> 00:01:15,737
.xml, so we use sitemap.xml.

19
00:01:15,737 --> 00:01:20,006
The text before it is the Base url from Settings, General.

20
00:01:20,458 --> 00:01:23,402
Check Enabled, so the index is served.

21
00:01:23,402 --> 00:01:27,215
Under Contained Sitemaps, check Articles and Posts.

22
00:01:27,215 --> 00:01:30,828
An index contains sitemaps, never other indexes.

23
00:01:31,284 --> 00:01:33,055
Save creates the index.

24
00:01:33,055 --> 00:01:37,755
The list shows Main as Enabled, with the buttons View, Edit and Delete.

25
00:01:37,755 --> 00:01:42,394
Edit lets you change its name, permalink and contained sitemaps later.

26
00:01:46,694 --> 00:01:49,153
View opens the index in another tab.

27
00:01:49,153 --> 00:01:53,777
For each contained sitemap, it gives an address and a last modified date,

28
00:01:53,777 --> 00:01:56,704
and a search engine follows those addresses.

29
00:01:57,157 --> 00:01:59,923
Each address leads to one sitemap.

30
00:01:59,923 --> 00:02:07,667
Posts lists each published blog post, with its address, last modified date, change frequency and priority.

31
00:02:08,140 --> 00:02:14,528
With the SEO feature enabled, Settings, Search, Search Engine Optimization, Robots has a

32
00:02:14,528 --> 00:02:17,043
checkbox, Include all sitemaps.

33
00:02:17,043 --> 00:02:19,761
Keep it checked and save the page.

34
00:02:19,761 --> 00:02:25,130
With a Base url set, your sitemaps are then added to the robots.txt file.

35
00:02:25,677 --> 00:02:30,991
The robots.txt file names an enabled index instead of each sitemap inside it,

36
00:02:30,991 --> 00:02:35,537
so crawlers learn about every sitemap in the index from one line.

37
00:02:39,737 --> 00:02:43,949
Building a sitemap takes work, so Orchard Core builds it the first time

38
00:02:43,949 --> 00:02:46,738
someone requests it, and stores it as a file.

39
00:02:46,738 --> 00:02:50,347
The file name is the sitemap name followed by an identifier.

40
00:02:51,097 --> 00:02:55,974
Saving a sitemap, or publishing content that it lists, changes its identifier.

41
00:02:55,974 --> 00:03:00,329
The next request builds it again under a different file name, and the

42
00:03:00,329 --> 00:03:02,477
old file stays on disk, unused.

43
00:03:06,777 --> 00:03:13,075
Under Tools, Search Engine Optimization, Sitemaps Cache lists every cached file by its file name.

44
00:03:13,075 --> 00:03:17,477
Here are the index, the Articles sitemap, and two files for Posts.

45
00:03:17,953 --> 00:03:22,580
Publishing a blog post changes the identifier of the Posts sitemap, so one

46
00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:24,952
of its two files is no longer used.

47
00:03:24,952 --> 00:03:28,133
Only a purge, or the cleanup feature, deletes it.

48
00:03:28,689 --> 00:03:31,889
Purge on a row deletes that file, after you confirm.

49
00:03:31,889 --> 00:03:34,979
Let's purge the Posts file that is no longer used.

50
00:03:35,445 --> 00:03:39,121
Purge All deletes every cached file, after you confirm.

51
00:03:39,121 --> 00:03:40,447
Nothing is lost:

52
00:03:40,447 --> 00:03:44,305
each sitemap is built again the next time it is requested.

53
00:03:48,605 --> 00:03:52,902
Instead of purging by hand, enable the Sitemaps Cleanup feature.

54
00:03:52,902 --> 00:03:58,305
Under Tools, Features, search for sitemaps, and choose Enable on Sitemaps Cleanup.

55
00:03:58,781 --> 00:04:05,945
With the Background Tasks feature enabled, Tools, Background Tasks lists a task named Sitemap Cache Cleaner.

56
00:04:05,945 --> 00:04:10,721
By default it runs every five minutes, and deletes cache files that no

57
00:04:10,721 --> 00:04:12,481
sitemap or index uses.

58
00:04:14,481 --> 00:04:18,800
A sitemap index gives search engines one address for all your sitemaps, and

59
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:23,705
the Sitemaps Cache holds their files until you purge them or the cleanup removes them.

60
00:04:23,705 --> 00:04:25,091
Thanks for watching.

